# Line endings are a property of this repository, not of a contributor's machine.
#
# The tree was authored on Windows and currently holds a mix: 70 files with CRLF and 114
# with LF. That normalises correctly today only because this machine happens to have
# `core.autocrlf=true` set globally -- a contributor without it would commit CRLF, and the
# Linux CI runner would then see a diff that does not exist locally.
#
# `text=auto` makes the normalisation a rule of the repository instead: every text file is
# stored as LF and checked out in the platform's native form.
* text=auto

# Shell scripts are the exception, and they need LF in the WORKING TREE, not only in the
# repository. `text=auto` checks a file out with CRLF on Windows, which is harmless for a
# config file and fatal for a script: frontend/docker-entrypoint.d/25-resolver.sh is copied
# verbatim into the nginx image, and a CRLF shebang makes the kernel look for an interpreter
# whose name ends in a carriage return. The image entrypoint would then report a failure that
# the build itself never showed.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the checkout form regardless of platform or of a contributor's core.autocrlf.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Data files whose sha256 is compared against a committed manifest. `text=auto` would check
# them out with CRLF on Windows and LF on Linux, so the bytes on disk -- and therefore the
# digest -- would depend on the platform, and the check would fail on exactly one of them.
# `-text` pins the checkout form to whatever was committed.
ml/data/processed/*.csv -text
